    "lawCode": "Amd §105, CPLR",
    "lawSection": "Civil Practice Law and Rules",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S586\n\nSPONSOR: KRUEGER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the civil practice law and rules, in relation to includ-\ning medical services, student loans, auto loans and retail installment\ncontracts in the definition of \"consumer credit transaction\"\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends subdivision f of section 105 of the civil practice law\nand rules by including debt incurred in connection with medical services\nand student loans in the definition of consumer credit transaction\n\nSection 2 provides the effective date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCurrently, all lawsuits filed by the State of New York for the\ncollection of student loan, debt owed are brought in Albany County.\nThis poses a significant burden on the student loan borrowers in areas\naway from Albany to defend a lawsuit. It is not only a matter of having\nto travel to Albany County to defend the case in person, but complicates\naccessing potentially information located only in Albany County. It also\nposes a problem for legal services lawyers and particularly lawyers\nworking for Legal Services Corporation programs because they don't\nrepresent individuals in jurisdictions outside their service area.\n\nNY CPLR sec. 503 requires a plaintiff to bring a case in the county or\ncity of the defendant's residence. Student debt (and medical debt) owed\nto New York State are exempt from the definition of a consumer credit\ntransaction under CPLR sec. 105(f) because they are not considered debt\n\"for personal, family or household purposes\" under the statute. This\nbill eliminates that exemption for consumers' benefit.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2021-2021- Referred to judiciary/A.3055 Referred to judiciary\n\n2019-2020- Referred to Rules/A10767- Referred to judiciary\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately after becoming law.\n",
