    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §503, CPLR",
    "lawSection": "Civil Practice Law and Rules",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S586A\n\nSPONSOR: KRUEGER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the civil practice law and rules, in relation to estab-\nlishing venue in actions to recover student debt\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends section 503 of the civil practice law and rules by\nadding a new section g\n\nSection 2 provides the effective date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNY CPLR section 503 requires a plaintiff to bring a case in the county\nor city of the defendant's residence. Student debt owed to New York\nState is exempt from the definition of a consumer credit transaction\nunder CPLR sec. 105(f) because they are not considered debt \"for\npersonal, family or household purposes\" under the statute. This bill\nadds a new section to section 503 that applies the same venue require-\nments in consumer credit transactions to cases where students owe debt\nto New York State to give them the opportunity to present a defense\nwithout having the burden of traveling to a distant court.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2022: A3055/S915 - referred to judiciary/ referred to judiciary\n\n2020-21: A10767/S08754 - referred to judiciary/REFERRED TO RULES\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately after becoming law.\n",
